  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Define Thomae's function on R and prove its Lebesgue integral over R and over [0, 1] is zero.",
    "plain": "Formalize the modified Dirichlet, or Thomae, function: it is nonzero only on rational points, so it is zero almost everywhere and has Lebesgue integral zero.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Thomae's function is a classic example separating pointwise discontinuity behavior from Lebesgue integration.",
      "The proof shows how a countable support gives an almost-everywhere zero function.",
      "The result reuses the same measure-zero rational-set idea behind the standard Dirichlet-function integral."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "Thomae's function is defined by rational denominator when x is rational and zero when x is irrational (Lean: thomae).",
      "The function is zero at irrational points, nonnegative, and bounded above by one (Lean: thomae_irrational, thomae_nonneg, thomae_le_one).",
      "The support of Thomae's function is contained in the rationals, hence it is zero almost everywhere (Lean: thomae_support_subset_rationals, thomae_ae_zero).",
      "The Lebesgue integral over R and over the unit interval is zero (Lean: thomae_integral_zero, thomae_integral_unit_interval).",
      "The file compares Thomae's function with the standard Dirichlet indicator and proves the Dirichlet integral is also zero (Lean: thomae_le_dirichlet, dirichlet_integral_zero)."
